{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-7350550f-c34f-45f2-8b96-4a91db5b3fb2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-a0bbfd7eecf9ffc2","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-9020-dgnyr0rz/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9020-decomposition.md","content":"# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9020\n\nAMD GPU (ROCm) enablement for serving and training.\n\n## Epic List\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n| E001 | AMD Instinct validation campaign: vLLM serving functional and performance parity | Investigation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n| E002 | Productize supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime for validated models | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n| E003 | PyTorch ROCm training support for single- and multi-GPU jobs | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n| E004 | Kueue amd.com/gpu quota handling for AMD Instinct nodes | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n| E005 | Document supported AMD hardware, driver prerequisites, and validated model list | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Serving | P1 |\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[E001 validation campaign] --> E002[E002 vLLM ROCm runtime]\n    E004[E004 Kueue amd.com/gpu quota] --> E003[E003 PyTorch ROCm training]\n    E001 --> E005[E005 documentation]\n    E002 --> E005\n    E003 --> E005\n    E004 --> E005\n```\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|------|------|-----------|\n| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The campaign's go/no-go outcome determines which models the productized runtime supports and whether the epic exists at all — a true gate (`rewrite`: scope narrows to the cleared model set; if the campaign is a full no-go, the epic is removed). |\n| E004 → E003 | Rule 12 (framework → consumer) | PyTorch training support is defined as jobs *admitted through Kueue* with amd.com/gpu quota; the quota/admission handling is the surface the training support is built and verified against (training-operator architecture context lists Kueue admission via kueue.x-k8s.io as an interface). |\n| E001 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) + content dependency | The validated model list documented by E005 is the campaign's deliverable. |\n| E002 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring blocked by ALL Implementation epics) | Docs describe what was built. |\n| E003 → E005 | Rule 11 | Same. |\n| E004 → E005 | Rule 11 | Same. |\n| E001 ∥ E004 | Rule 14 (no shared artifacts) | Serving validation and Kueue quota work share no artifacts; they proceed in parallel. |\n| E002 ∥ E003, E004 | Rule 14 | Different components/repos, no shared artifacts. |\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n|-----|----------|---------|\n| HLR-1 | P0 | E001 |\n| HLR-2 | P0 | E002 |\n| HLR-3 | P1 | E003, E004 |\n| HLR-4 | P2 | E005 |\n\nAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements. HLR-3 spans two components (Training Operator: job/runtime support; Distributed Workloads: Kueue quota handling) and is covered by two epics per the component boundary rule. HLR-4 is P2 in the strategy, but the docs-authoring epic's priority derives from the strategy frontmatter `priority: Major` → P1 per the docs-authoring priority exception (it does not push priority upstream to any implementation).\n\n## Component Mapping Notes\n\n- All epic `component` values are copied verbatim from `.context/rhai-components.txt`. No non-obvious mappings were needed.\n- **KServe (passive):** the strategy lists KServe as affected but describes no KServe-specific change — the vLLM ROCm runtime executes under existing KServe InferenceServices, and KServe requires no code change for a new runtime image. Per the passive-component rule, KServe validation (\"serving works through a KServe InferenceService on AMD Instinct nodes\") is an acceptance criterion on E001 (campaign) and E002 (productized runtime), owned by the same team (RHOAI Serving), so no separate validation epic is generated.\n- All four strategy components exist in `.context/architecture-context/` (all Stable/GA, all Konflux-onboarded) — no onboarding chain is needed, and no new-component provisioning epics are generated.\n- Team assignments were derived from the architecture-context owning teams (RHOAI Serving: KServe, Serving Runtimes; Distributed Workloads: Training Operator, Distributed Workloads) because the strategy contains no Impacted Teams table.\n\n## Open Questions Disposition\n\n| Open question | Disposition | Rationale |\n|---------------|-------------|-----------|\n| Hardware access — dedicated AMD Instinct nodes; procurement or partner-lab access unresolved | Risk / Health Warning on E001 and E003, not an epic or AC | An availability precondition granted by parties outside the strategy. It does not change which epics exist or what they do — the campaign and training-validation epics exist either way — so it is not an Investigation. ACs are written on the deliverables (the validated report, the validated training results); securing access is epic work noted in Risks. |\n| Upstream ROCm build cadence — AMD's runtime image releases may not align with release trains | Risk on E002 / Health Warning | Externally-controlled timing, not a technical unknown the team can resolve, and it does not change epic structure: productization pins whichever ROCm image digest the campaign validated. Mitigated by the pinned-digest AC on E002. |\n| Which models the campaign clears (HLR-1 → HLR-2 gating) | E001 Investigation gating E002 | The answer changes what E002 does (supported model scope) and whether it exists (full no-go). Outcomes form a continuum (cleared model set), not ≤3 bounded branches, so a single gated Implementation with `gate_failure_impact` is used instead of branch files. |\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- **Missing strategy sections:** the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Acceptance Criteria (Given/When/Then), and no Non-Functional Requirements. Decomposition proceeded with available information; team assignments were derived from architecture-context owning teams, and ACs were derived from HLRs. A dependency on physical AMD Instinct hardware is a stated platform prerequisite, not a strategy-level dependency table entry.\n- **Availability precondition (hardware):** the validation campaign and training validation both require dedicated AMD Instinct nodes that do not exist in the CI fleet; procurement or partner-lab access is unresolved and externally controlled. This gates calendar timing of E001 and E003 but is not modeled as a DAG edge or an AC. If no hardware access is secured, E001/E003 cannot execute and the strategy stalls — a program-level risk, not a decomposition defect.\n- **External cadence risk:** upstream ROCm image releases are controlled by AMD and may not align with platform release trains; E002 mitigates by pinning the campaign-validated image digest, at the cost of shipping an older ROCm base than upstream latest.\n- **Shared-hardware coordination:** E001 (serving validation) and E003 (training validation) both consume the same scarce AMD Instinct nodes; they are DAG-parallel but will contend for hardware — a coordination risk for planning, not a dependency.\n- **Priority inversion check:** none found. HLR priorities (P0 serving validation+productization, P1 training, P2 docs) are consistent with the strategy's emphasis on serving first for the two strategic customers.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nThe external dependency (AMD/upstream ROCm image cadence, uncertain timing) admits tiered delivery:\n\n- **Tier 1 (independent value, start immediately):** E001 (parity data and go/no-go scope — risk-reduction value on its own), E004 (Kueue quota handling — independently useful), E003 (training support, verifiable on whatever ROCm images upstream currently publishes).\n- **Tier 2 (gated):** E002 — productization is gated by the campaign outcome (and practically by which ROCm image the campaign validated). E005 (docs) follows all implementations.\n\nIf the campaign no-goes entirely, Tier 2 collapses to documentation of the negative result and HLR-2 is dropped.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32424,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9897d4d4516b5fdf","agent_id":"a94ae0bee30045f32","session_id":"5032c0b6-6c0b-415b-b4db-8fcaacffdca3","uuid":"73e9b760-fa24-4af0-a5b8-dc0258e39f4a","timestamp":"2026-10-26T20:36:25.740Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-9020"}
