{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9c842c78ab00b6e0","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9020-E001\n3\ttitle: 'AMD Instinct validation campaign: vLLM serving functional and performance\n4\t  parity'\n5\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9020\n6\tcomponent: Serving Runtimes\n7\tteam: RHOAI Serving\n8\ttype: Investigation\n9\tpriority: P0\n10\tdependencies: []\n11\tinvestigation_signals:\n12\t  question_specificity: 1\n13\t  source_accessibility: 0\n14\t  local_runnability: 0\n15\t  cluster_hardware_dependence: -2\n16\t  human_judgment_required: -1\n17\timplementation_type: null\n18\tai_signals: null\n19\tai_implementability: Low\n20\tai_implementability_score: -2\n21\tjira_key: null\n22\tbranch: null\n23\tgated_by: null\n24\tgate_failure_impact: null\n25\t---\n26\t# AMD Instinct validation campaign: vLLM serving functional and performance parity\n27\t\n28\t## Description\n29\t\n30\tThis investigation resolves the central unknown of the AMD GPU (ROCm) enablement strategy: whether vLLM serving on AMD Instinct accelerators reaches functional and performance parity with the platform's CUDA builds for the supported model set. It runs a validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct hardware and produces the parity report and per-model go/no-go scope that determines which models the productized ROCm serving runtime will support. The campaign's outcome gates the downstream productization work: a model that clears becomes supported; a model that fails is excluded (or, on a full no-go, productization does not proceed).\n31\t\n32\t## Scope\n33\t\n34\t- Stand up the validation environment on dedicated AMD Instinct nodes (securing procurement or partner-lab access is part of this epic's work; the access itself is an externally-granted precondition tracked in Risks).\n35\t- Run vLLM serving for every model in the supported model set using upstream ROCm runtime images, exercising the platform's serving path end to end (models served through KServe InferenceServices, which require no code change for a ROCm runtime image).\n36\t- Record functional results per model: correct loading, inference correctness, and error behavior on ROCm versus the CUDA baseline.\n37\t- Record performance parity data per model: throughput and latency against the CUDA baseline on comparable hardware.\n38\t- Define and apply the pass/fail criteria for functional correctness and performance parity thresholds.\n39\t- Produce the campaign report: per-model results, the go/no-go scope for productization, and the exact ROCm image digests validated.\n40\t\n41\t## Acceptance Criteria\n42\t\n43\t- The campaign report covers every model in the supported model set with an explicit functional pass/fail result for vLLM serving on ROCm.\n44\t- The campaign report records performance parity data (throughput and latency versus the CUDA baseline) for every model in the supported model set.\n45\t- The campaign report documents the go/no-go scope for productization: the list of models cleared for the supported ROCm serving runtime, with the decision criteria applied.\n46\t- The campaign report records the exact ROCm runtime image digests used for the validated results.\n47\t- Serving during the campaign is exercised through KServe InferenceServices on AMD Instinct nodes (KServe kept working, no code change).\n48\t\n49\t## HLR Traceability\n50\t\n51\t- HLR-1 (P0): A validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct hardware establishes functional and performance parity data for vLLM serving of the supported model set, producing the go/no-go scope for productization.\n52\t\n53\t## Risks / Notes\n54\t\n55\t- Hardware access is an availability precondition granted by parties outside this strategy (procurement or partner lab); it is unresolved at decomposition time and is not controlled by this epic's engineering work. If access is delayed, the campaign schedule slips accordingly — this is a program-level risk, not a deliverable condition.\n56\t- Upstream ROCm runtime image cadence is controlled by AMD and the upstream communities; the campaign pins the image digests it validates and is therefore insensitive to later upstream releases, but a mid-campaign upstream breaking change would require re-validation.\n57\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-90b3178d6091b204","agent_id":"a4731f30b686527e0","session_id":"5032c0b6-6c0b-415b-b4db-8fcaacffdca3","uuid":"62f98a99-9b1b-4f87-a244-3ff1b8fc6706","timestamp":"2026-10-26T20:38:34.507Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-9c842c78ab00b6e0","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9020 decomposition"}
